## Group Aggregates

A group row can be in view while the rows it aggregates are not. Take five sector groups of 20 stocks each, with every group collapsed. Five group rows are in view, and `subscribeRows` is called with those five rows only.

With the Server-Side Row Model, the server calculates the group rows. It can keep each group's aggregates current as its stocks change, and send the recalculated group row to the client. The client then needs updates for the five group rows only, and doesn't load any of the 100 stocks until a group is expanded.

This differs from the Client-Side Row Model, where the grid calculates the group rows from the stocks it holds, and so needs updates for all 100 stocks to keep the five totals current. See [Group Aggregates](./data-update-view-subscription/#group-aggregates).

The [pivoting example](#pivoting) below sends whole group rows. With pivot mode on, **Subscribed rows** and **Streamed rows** both show 5, while the server tracks the 100 stocks behind them.

### What the Counters Measure

The examples count three different things:

- **Subscribed rows**: the rows passed to `subscribeRows` that are not yet unsubscribed. There is one subscription for each row in view.
- **Streamed rows**: the rows the server streams updates for because of those subscriptions. The server sends each subscribed group row whole, so there is one streamed row for each subscribed row.
- **Updates sent**: the number of rows the server has sent in updates so far. It keeps rising, as a streamed row is updated many times.

Every example sends all of its subscriptions and updates over one simulated websocket. Streaming rows for many subscriptions doesn't take many connections: one connection carries the updates for every subscription.

## Pivoting

In [pivot mode](./server-side-model-pivoting/), each row in view is a group row, passed with `type: 'group'`. Each of its cells aggregates the leaf rows under it for one pivot result column. A subscription covers the whole row, including result columns scrolled out of view, so the server sends updates for everything the row aggregates.

Turning pivot mode on or off, or changing the pivot columns, reloads the rows. Every row is unsubscribed, and the reloaded rows are subscribed as their blocks load.

The example below has the same stocks, controls and pausing rules as the [Client-Side Row Model pivoting example](./data-update-view-subscription/#pivoting), with these differences:

- The server groups, pivots and aggregates the stocks, and sends the pivot result fields with each block, so the grid creates the pivot result columns. `getRowId` gives group rows an ID from their route.
- The server sends each subscribed group row whole, recalculated whenever one of its stocks moves, so **Streamed rows** equals **Subscribed rows** while no stream is paused.
- The updates are applied with [Asynchronous Transactions](./server-side-model-updating-transactions/#asynchronous-updates), using each row's `parentKeys` as the route.

{% gridExampleRunner title="View Subscription with Server-Side Pivoting" name="ssrm-pivot-view-subscription" exampleHeight=615 /%}

